## What this loop is

A photograph goes in. RF-DETR reads keypoints out of it, ANNY is fitted to those
keypoints, the fitted rig is rendered, and the render is scored against the photograph.
The referee is a second opinion answering a question EditScore cannot: whether a body
could hold that pose at all.

Nothing in this notebook runs until you run it. Every cell that touches the card is a
subprocess into a pixi environment, so reading the notebook costs nothing.

## Two facts that will bite before anything else does

**The detector is the fullbody one, so the vocabulary is ANNY's 104 bone labels.** An
earlier version of this notebook subset every target to COCO-17 and then reported that the
referee could not run. It could not run because 87 of the 104 points were being thrown
away. Nothing here asserts a length now: names arrive with the targets, and a name the
forward does not carry raises rather than shortening the array.

**`render_corpus.py` requires the 19,158-vertex makehuman topology** with
`remove_unattached_vertices=False`. The 13,718-vertex body submodel that `anny_rig.py`
builds for the corpus is a different width, and `coco.pth` is indexed against the first.
One direction fails loudly. The other does not, which is the dangerous one.

